# {{project_name}} — GitHub Copilot instructions

This file provides guidance to GitHub Copilot when working with code in this repository.

## Project root and harness contract

`project_root` is the actual bootstrapped project folder, which may be nested below the
Git root. Resolve it once at run start: use the explicit absolute project folder supplied
by the caller, or the session working directory if it contains
`.github/.bootstrap-manifest.json`. If neither identifies it, ask for the project folder;
do not guess from the Git root or search for an unrelated ancestor's `.github`.
Confirm the marker and this instructions file belong to that folder.

`harness_root` is the absolute `project_root/.github` directory. All local paths in these
instructions, plans and file lists are **project-root-relative** unless explicitly absolute.
Use native OS path separators when constructing paths. Run project commands at
`project_root` unless an approved plan supplies a project-relative working directory.
Do not persist machine-specific absolute roots in generated templates or committed plans.

The coordinator passes absolute `project_root`, `harness_root`, and `artifact_root` to
every worker. Workers use those inputs, never independently re-derive roots from their cwd.
Read `<harness_root>/copilot-instructions.md` explicitly before stage work; also read
`<harness_root>/instructions/tests.instructions.md` for planning/testing. Markdown links
and `#file` references can aid discovery but do not guarantee content injection.
If instructions are unreadable or roots disagree, return `status: blocked`, not a guess.

## Shared stage-result contract

Each worker is terminal: do its assigned stage and return to the same coordinator.
Never invoke another agent, an orchestrator, a built-in task agent, or an alias.
Only the coordinator fetches tickets. Stage payloads, not ambient ticket access, determine
what each worker receives.

Return these four fields for both normal work and qualification:

```text
status: passed|blocked|failed
outputs: stage-specific plans, file lists, findings or proposals; include partial results
evidence: actual reads/edits, command + cwd + exit code + result, or review references
blocker: none when passed; otherwise kind, detail, and required resolution
```

`passed` requires that stage's actual output and evidence, not an acknowledgement.
`blocked` means a missing decision, plan detail, authentication, tool capability or other
prerequisite; `failed` means an attempted stage did not meet its gate (including red tests
or verification FAIL). Preserve exact relevant errors; never fabricate execution evidence
or hide partial changes. Report missing result fields as blocked, not implicit success.

Execution workers also report `skill_source: injected|read|unavailable` and the skill's
name/path, accurately describing how they obtained the full content. If unavailable, block.
An injected skill is usable; an explicit read is the reliable fallback, not a failure.
No mandatory claim that it was "preloaded".

For `mode: qualification`, do only the coordinator's artifact-only capability probe and
return its evidence; do not start feature work. In that mode, read-only workers read the
supplied canary and common instructions and demonstrate search with an actual tool call
scoped to `harness_root`; record the path/query/result and errors. They never edit or execute.
Normal stage calls read their instructions and supplied stage inputs, not deleted canaries.
This proves only those observed operations at that dispatch depth, not all future permissions.

